---
/*
 * The ruled field the whole site sits on.
 *
 * Every card, rule and annotation on this design is aligned to an implied grid.
 * This draws it: a fine construction grid and a coarse datum grid over it, the
 * way a drawing sheet is ruled before anything is drawn on it.
 *
 * It does not move, and that is the point. Parallax needs a cursor position, CSS
 * cannot read one, and reading one costs JavaScript on every page of a site that
 * otherwise ships none. The static grid was what was actually on screen while
 * the parallax was silently broken — and it was the static grid that looked
 * right. It was not worth a script to animate something already working.
 *
 * It sits at z-index -1, which puts it above the body background — that
 * background propagates to the canvas and paints first — and below every piece
 * of ordinary content, without wrapping the page in anything.
 *
 * That last part matters more than it sounds. A wrapper with a z-index makes a
 * stacking context, and a stacking context traps `mix-blend-mode`: the cover
 * mark is composited against whatever is behind it, and inside a wrapper the
 * field is not behind it any more. The artwork's ground reappeared as a white
 * panel the moment a wrapper existed. No wrapper, no trap, and the grid reads
 * straight through the mark.
 *
 * Cards carry their own ground and occlude it, so the field shows through the
 * gutters and margins and stops at the edge of anything solid — which is the
 * correct reading: it is the paper, not a texture laid over the top.
 */
---

<div class="grid-field" data-grid-field aria-hidden="true"></div>

<style>
  .grid-field {
    position: fixed;
    /*
     * Overscanned well past the viewport so the parallax can never drag an edge
     * of the gradient into view at the extremes of travel.
     */
    inset: -8vmax;
    z-index: -1;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .grid-field::before,
  .grid-field::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background-image:
      repeating-linear-gradient(
        to right,
        var(--rule-ink) 0 1px,
        transparent 1px var(--cell)
      ),
      repeating-linear-gradient(
        to bottom,
        var(--rule-ink) 0 1px,
        transparent 1px var(--cell)
      );
  }

  /* The construction grid: fine and barely there. */
  .grid-field::before {
    --cell: 34px;
    --rule-ink: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-rule) 42%, transparent);
  }

  /* The datum grid: coarse, a shade stronger, every fifth rule. */
  .grid-field::after {
    --cell: 170px;
    --rule-ink: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-rule) 78%, transparent);
  }

  /* On a phone the fine grid is denser than the type it sits behind. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .grid-field::before {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
